## Why This Is Asked
Tests DP setup for segment selection with non-adjacency and reconstruction, a common beginner DP pattern with a twist.

## Key Concepts
- DP over index i and blocks t
- Enforce non-adjacency via s-2 reference
- Prefix sums for O(1) subarray sums
- Reconstruction pointers for endpoints
- Time/space trade-offs; possible optimization to O(nk) time and O(n) space per k

## Code Example
```javascript
function maxKSubarraysSum(A, k) {
  const n = A.length;
  const P = new Array(n + 1).fill(0);
  for (let i = 0; i < n; i++) P[i + 1] = P[i] + A[i];

  const dp = Array.from({ length: n + 1 }, () => Array(k + 1).fill(-Infinity));
  const take = Array.from({ length: n + 1 }, () => Array(k + 1).fill(-1));
  dp[0][0] = 0;

  for (let i = 1; i <= n; i++) {
    for (let t = 0; t <= k; t++) {
      // skip i
      dp[i][t] = Math.max(dp[i][t], dp[i - 1][t]);
      if (t > 0) {
        for (let s = 1; s <= i; s++) {
          const sum = P[i] - P[s - 1];
          const prev = (s >= 2) ? dp[s - 2][t - 1] : (t - 1 === 0 ? 0 : -Infinity);
          const cand = sum + prev;
          if (cand > dp[i][t]) {
            dp[i][t] = cand;
            take[i][t] = s;
          }
        }
      }
    }
  }
  // reconstruction would trace back using 'take' from (n, bestT)
  return { maxSum: dp[n][0], blocks: [] };
}
```

## Why This Is Asked
This problem tests DP formulation for selecting multiple non-overlapping subarrays with reconstruction—a common pattern in budget allocation and resource optimization scenarios.

## Key Concepts
- **DP over prefixes and block counts**: State `dp[i][t]` tracks the optimal solution using only the first i elements for exactly t blocks.
- **Prefix sums**: Enable O(1) computation of any subarray sum as `pref[r] - pref[l-1]`.
- **Backpointers**: Store decision history to reconstruct the actual subarray boundaries after computing the DP table.
- **Time/space complexity**: Baseline O(n²k) time and O(nk) space; can be optimized with divide-and-conquer or convex hull tricks for larger inputs.

## Code Example
```javascript
function maxKSubarrays(A, k) {
  const n = A.length;
  const pref = new Array(n + 1).fill(0);
  for (let i = 1; i <= n; i++) pref[i] = pref[i - 1] + A[i - 1];

  const dp = Array.from({ length: n + 1 }, () => new Array(k + 1).fill(-Infinity));
  const choice = Array.from({ length: n + 1 }, () => new Array(k + 1).fill(null));
  dp[0][0] = 0;

  for (let i = 1; i <= n; i++) {
    for (let t = 1; t <= k; t++) {
      dp[i][t] = dp[i - 1][t];
      choice[i][t] = { type: 'skip', i: i - 1 };

      for (let s = 1; s <= i; s++) {
        const val = dp[s - 1][t - 1] + pref[i] - pref[s - 1];
        if (val > dp[i][t]) {
          dp[i][t] = val;
          choice[i][t] = { type: 'start', s, i };
        }
      }
    }
  }

  // Reconstruct boundaries
  const blocks = [];
  let i = n, t = k;
  while (t > 0 && i > 0) {
    const c = choice[i][t];
    if (c.type === 'start') {
      blocks.unshift([c.s, c.i]);
      i = c.s - 1;
    } else {
      i = c.i;
    }
    t--;
  }

  return { maxSum: dp[n][k], boundaries: blocks };
}
```

## Why This Is Asked
Tests DP over partitioning with a quadratic segment cost, requiring prefix sums, base cases, and reconstruction, plus discussion of optimizations.

## Key Concepts
- DP over partitions: dp[i][t] for first i elements into t segments
- Prefix sums for O(1) segment sums
